  "sourceType": "CLASSIC",
  "sourceNotes": "The chiffon cake was invented by Harry Baker, a Los Angeles insurance salesman and amateur baker, who sold his recipe to General Mills in 1947. It was introduced to the American public in 1948 in a Better Homes and Gardens feature as 'the first really new cake in 100 years.' The technique of using vegetable oil and folded whipped egg whites is the defining innovation. This coconut and lime variant follows the standard chiffon formula while drawing on the tropical-flavoured chiffon cakes popular in Southeast Asia (particularly the Philippines and Thailand) from the 1970s onward.",
